{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born in 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. After his father's death in 1152 Raymond II was succeeded by his son Raymond III who was still a minor at the time. \n\n*Early Life and Regency* \nRaymond's early life was marked by the regency of his mother Hodierna and her sister Melisende the Queen of Jerusalem. In 1155 Raymond came of age and took control of the County of Tripoli. He was a capable leader and worked to strengthen his county and secure its borders. In 1160 Raymond was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi the ruler of Aleppo and was held for nearly ten years. During his captivity the County of Tripoli was governed by a regent.\n\n*Count of Tripoli* \nAfter his release in 1174 Raymond returned to Tripoli and resumed his duties as count. He worked to rebuild his county and restore its economy. Raymond also played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a member of the Haute Cour the high court of the kingdom and was involved in several important decisions. In 1174 Raymond supported the accession of Baldwin IV to the throne of Jerusalem. \n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Death* \nIn 1187 Raymond led the vanguard of the Crusader army at the Battle of Hattin against the forces of Saladin. The Crusaders were defeated and Raymond was one of the few leaders who escaped the battle. He fled to Tyre and later to Tripoli where he died soon after arriving. Raymond's death marked the end of an era in the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. Raymond III was a significant figure in the history of the Crusades and his 